<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Phra Kaew</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Temple of the Emerald Buddha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Phra Kaew, the Temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a>, is the royal temple within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. Built by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> from 1782 and consecrated in 1784, it houses the Emerald Buddha, the most venerated image in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, whose robes the king changes with the seasons three times a year. Its gilded buildings, guardian giants and murals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakien">Ramakien</a> make it the foremost Buddhist temple of the country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-phra-kaew.jpg" alt="Wat Phra Kaew" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original image: Ninara from Helsinki, Finland. Edit: TSP · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Phra_Kaew_by_Ninara_TSP_edit_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
